    ## Capability Gaps
  - (see Research Findings — updated after research)
  + - **OS-level confinement** is still the biggest structural gap. Codex runs every command under a permission profile: writes inside the workspace, `.git`/`.codex` read-only, network off by default, and a `codex sandbox` CLI to test a profile. yoyo decides on command text + a tree-sitter pass (#1202) + directory fences, and nothing stops a command it approves from writing `.git/hooks` or reaching the network. Large, not one session; #1202 is the incremental path.
  + - **Trust vs sandbox separation**: Codex 0.169 keeps project config + MCP disabled in untrusted dirs under `codex exec`, even with a writable sandbox, and adds an explicit `--trust`. yoyo gates project hooks/permissions/commands/notify by trust, but #1188 (trust keyed on path, not content: a `git pull` that adds `base_url` to a trusted `.yoyo.toml` is honoured silently) is the open hole in that model.
  + - Persistent background/scheduled work: Claude Code has `/loop`, scheduled tasks and a unified `/tasks` view; yoyo has /bg and /spawn (#1018 parked).
  + - Recursive deletes of root/home/cwd/top-level dirs: Claude Code 2.1.330 just widened this. yoyo already flags home, `$HOME`, `.`/`..` and system dirs (safety.rs tests ~2757-3437), so it's not a gap.
  + - Self-test incidence: this assessment's own heredoc (python3 rewriting this file, with a sentence *mentioning* a home-dir recursive delete) was hard-blocked: one more #1102 hit, Day 266 21:4x.
